## 3. THEORETICAL CONCEPTS & VULNERABILITY MECHANICS

### A. Vulnerable Architecture (CWE-89)
In insecure applications, dynamic SQL statements are constructed by concatenating unsanitized user inputs directly into the SQL interpreter string:
$$\text{User Input} \longrightarrow \text{Direct String Interpolation} \longrightarrow \text{Database Query Interpreter}$$
When untrusted characters (such as `'`, `--`, `OR`, `UNION`) are supplied, the database compiler misinterprets user data as SQL control commands, altering query logic.

### B. Secure Architecture (Remediated)
In hardened applications, data is strictly separated from query instructions:
$$\text{User Input} \longrightarrow \text{Regex / Type Validation} \longrightarrow \text{Prepared Statement Binding (?)} \longrightarrow \text{Database}$$
Parameterized queries pre-compile the SQL statement structure. User parameters are transmitted separately as literal byte values, making SQL syntax alteration mathematically impossible.

